      "id": "R01",
      "abstract": "Distributed manipulation-data collection needs both scene variation and reproducible robot control. We present DROID, a manipulation dataset and collection platform built around shared portable Franka Panda hardware at 13 institutions. Collectors calibrate cameras for each scene and teleoperate randomly assigned feasible tasks; periodic prompts change camera placement, lighting, clutter, or robot base position to broaden coverage. The dataset contains 76,000 successful episodes from 564 scenes, accompanied by crowdsourced language instructions. Collection instructions and policy-training resources support reuse of the platform and data. We evaluate the resource through separate language-conditioned diffusion policies for six tasks in four locations on DROID hardware. Equal-share co-training with in-domain demonstrations and successful DROID trajectories exceeds the next-best of in-domain-only training and equal-share co-training with a curated Open X-Embodiment mixture by 22 percentage points in mean in-distribution success and 17 under task-specific out-of-distribution changes. All methods share the same policy backbone. A fixed-size subset comparison also favors broader scene coverage on challenging shifted tasks, supporting scene diversity as a useful dimension of this supplementary training resource.",
      "methods": "DROID standardizes distributed collection through a portable Franka Panda platform with two adjustable external stereo cameras, a wrist stereo camera, and VR teleoperation. Collectors position and calibrate cameras in each scene, specify feasible tasks, and receive a randomly sampled task for each episode. Periodic prompts vary camera placement, lighting, clutter, or robot base position to promote coverage of varied conditions. For each of six tasks in four locations, a separate language-conditioned diffusion policy is trained with an equal mix of in-domain demonstrations and successful DROID trajectories; unsuccessful trajectories are excluded. The same backbone is evaluated after training on in-domain demonstrations alone or equal-share co-training with a curated Open X-Embodiment mixture. Each method and task setting receives ten rollouts on DROID hardware, both under perturbed initial robot and object positions within the demonstration distribution and under task-specific shifts involving distractors, different objects, or camera placement.",
      "results": "On six tasks in four locations, DROID co-training improved across-task mean success over the next-best comparator by 22 percentage points in-distribution and 17 under task-specific out-of-distribution shifts. All methods used the same diffusion-policy backbone; the alternatives were in-domain-only training and equal-share co-training with a curated Open X-Embodiment mixture. DROID co-training likewise mixed in-domain demonstrations and successful DROID trajectories equally. Each method and task setting used ten rollouts, with standard error reported around across-task means. A secondary comparison held subset size at 7,362 successful demonstrations and retained equal-share co-training. Uniformly sampled trajectories spanning more scenes outperformed trajectories from the 20 most represented scenes on the challenging shifted tasks studied. Thus the main comparison supports supplementary DROID data on the shared hardware, while the subset comparison supports a role for scene diversity without identifying it as the sole cause.",
      "notes": {
        "abstract": "Selected D01-D09 to connect reproducible collection, scene variation, and utility under unchanged diffusion policies. Read-back retains both the resource and platform contributions and interprets the gains as six-task co-training results on DROID hardware, not a new algorithm or replacement for in-domain demonstrations.",
        "methods": "D01-D02 and D05-D06 anchor the collection-to-training procedure; D03 anchors successful-trajectory selection and D07 the rollout protocol. Read-back recovers how random task assignment and periodic scene changes broaden intended coverage, and why shared-backbone comparators assess the training-data choice. Collection intent is not treated as an isolated cause of improved success.",
        "results": "D05-D08 anchor the main comparison and fixed-size scene-diversity check. Read-back separates rollout counts from task averages, preserves percentage-point units and the reported standard-error definition, and limits the subset result to the challenging shifted tasks examined. Neither comparison licenses sole-cause attribution to scene diversity."
      }
    },
    {
      "id": "R02",
      "abstract": "Control and visual discrepancies complicate simulated evaluation of policies trained on real robot data. We present SIMPLER, a framework for comparing relative policy performance and behavior rather than reproducing exact physical success rates. Existing demonstration actions are replayed in simulation, and controller stiffness and damping are tuned to reduce mismatch with real end-effector motion. Visual Matching combines simulated robots and interactive objects with an inpainted real-scene background, then adjusts textures to better match the real robot and objects. The framework implements Google Robot and WidowX setups in SAPIEN, primarily for rigid-body tasks. Mean Maximum Rank Violation evaluates policy ordering by penalizing errors according to real-world success-rate margins. Across six policy checkpoints and three Google Robot task groups, its average was 0.056 with Visual Matching, versus 0.143 for averaging randomized visual variants and 0.375 for validation action mean squared error. Real arm-wrapping experiments also supported simulation's indication that Octo-Base was more arm-texture-sensitive than RT-1-X. These findings support relative policy evaluation and behavioral comparisons in the tested setups.",
      "methods": "SIMPLER evaluates policies trained on real robot data by reducing discrepancies in simulated action effects and observations. System identification replays actions from existing real demonstrations, compares simulated and real end-effector translation and rotation, and uses simulated annealing to tune controller stiffness and damping. Visual Matching combines segmented simulated robots and interactive objects with an inpainted real-scene background, then adjusts or projects textures to match real appearances. Variant Aggregation instead averages outcomes across randomized visual variants. Existing policies are evaluated on real Google Robot and WidowX setups and their SAPIEN counterparts, mainly on rigid-body tasks. Mean Maximum Rank Violation assigns each policy its largest real-world success-rate margin among incorrectly ordered policy pairs and averages these penalties; lower values indicate closer ranking agreement. Pearson correlation supplies a complementary measure of linear association.",
      "results": "For six policy checkpoints across three Google Robot task groups, Visual Matching achieved better average agreement with real-world policy ordering than Variant Aggregation or validation action mean squared error. Mean Maximum Rank Violation, which penalizes incorrect ordering by the real-world success-rate margin, averaged 0.056, 0.143, and 0.375 for these approaches, respectively; lower values indicate closer agreement. The contrast favors direct appearance alignment over averaging randomized visual variants for this ranking task, while action prediction error supplied a weaker ranking signal. A complementary behavioral check tested simulation's suggestion that Octo-Base was more sensitive to arm texture than RT-1-X: new physical arm-wrapping experiments supported this relative sensitivity. Together, these results support simulation as a source of relative performance and robustness comparisons in the evaluated setups. Some policy checkpoints nevertheless remained sensitive to visual differences, so the agreement does not imply exact success-rate reproduction.",
      "notes": {
        "abstract": "S01-S05 and S07-S09 anchor the framework, gap-reduction procedures, margin-aware metric, and ranking/behavior evidence. Read-back retains these co-contributions while distinguishing relative agreement from absolute success-rate reproduction. The reported average concerns six checkpoints across three Google Robot task groups; the behavioral check concerns Octo-Base versus RT-1-X.",
        "methods": "S02-S06 anchor action replay, appearance alignment, variant averaging, and real/simulated evaluation. Read-back recovers each operation and defines the ranking penalty before interpreting its direction. Rigid-body task scope is retained; visual alignment is not equated with photorealistic backgrounds or new policy training.",
        "results": "S02, S07, and S09 anchor ranking agreement and the physical texture-sensitivity check; S08 supplies the residual visual-sensitivity qualification. Read-back treats MMRV as a margin-aware ranking measure, not success rate, and separates the Google Robot aggregate from the Octo-Base/RT-1-X behavioral comparison. No significance or exact-fidelity claim is made."
      }
    }
  ]
